    "result_text": "I implemented `summarize_ticket` in `app/summary.py`, and it passes both the existing tests and a separate check I ran against a fake model.\n\n```python\nmessages = [SystemMessage(content=SUMMARY_PROMPT), HumanMessage(content=ticket_text(ticket))]\nmessage = chat.invoke(messages, stop=[\"\\n\\n\"])\nreturn str(message.text).strip()\n```\n\n- **One request:** it makes a single `invoke` call with the system message and the user message, and passes the stop sequence `\"\\n\\n\"`. This works the same way on any LangChain chat model.\n- **Reply as text:** it reads the reply with `message.text` instead of `str(message.content)`. Some providers, such as Anthropic, return the reply as a list of pieces. `str(content)` would turn that list into a string like `\"[{'type': 'text', ...}]\"`, while `.text` joins the pieces into the actual text. The result is a plain `str` with leading and trailing whitespace removed.\n\n**Testing:** `pytest -q` passes (2 tests; neither covers `summarize_ticket`). For that, I wrote a separate check using a fake chat model. It confirmed there is exactly one call, the two messages are correct, the stop sequence is `[\"\\n\\n\"]`, and the output is a plain `str`. It passed with the reply given both as a plain string and as a list of pieces.\n\nI couldn't delete that check file because the `rm` was blocked pending approval. It's still at `.tmpcheck/test_summary_check.py`. You can delete it, or move it into `tests/` if you want to keep it as a real test.\n\nNo dependencies were added."
